T*
(* Virtualization: The abstraction of physical computer hardware via software,) Tj
T*
(enabling multiple isolated operating system environments to execute concurrently on a) Tj
T*
(single physical host:) Tj
T*
(* Type-1 Hypervisor \(Bare-Metal\): Runs directly on physical host hardware without an) Tj
T*
(underlying host OS. Maximum performance, lowest latency, enterprise standard \(VMware) Tj
T*
(ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, KVM, Xen\).) Tj
T*
(* Type-2 Hypervisor \(Hosted\): Runs as a software application on top of a standard) Tj
T*
(host OS. Higher overhead \(VirtualBox, VMware Workstation\).) Tj
T*
(* Containerization \(OS-Level Virtualization - Docker\):) Tj
T*
(- Core Paradigm: Containers share the host operating system kernel directly, packaging) Tj
T*
(only user-space binaries, libraries, and application dependencies.) Tj
T*
(- Linux Kernel Primitives: Namespaces \(providing isolated views of PID, Network, Mount) Tj
T*
(points, IPC\) and Cgroups \(enforcing resource limits on CPU, memory, I/O bandwidth\).) Tj
T*
(- Advantages over VMs: Lightweight \(megabytes vs gigabytes\), near-instantaneous startup) Tj
T*
(\(milliseconds vs minutes\), high server density, unified dev-to-prod parity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 6.3 Cybersecurity Fundamentals: The CIA Triad and Cryptography) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* The CIA Triad \(The Core Tenets of Information Security\):) Tj
T*
(- Confidentiality: Ensuring sensitive data is accessible strictly to authorized entities) Tj
T*
(\(enforced via Encryption, Access Control Lists\).) Tj
T*
(- Integrity: Ensuring information is accurate and has not been maliciously or) Tj
T*
(accidentally tampered with \(enforced via Cryptographic Hash Functions: SHA-256, HMAC\).) Tj
T*
(- Availability: Ensuring computing services and data remain accessible to authorized) Tj
T*
(users when needed \(enforced via Redundancy, DDoS Mitigation, Fault Tolerance\).) Tj
T*
(* Cryptographic Paradigms:) Tj
T*
(- Symmetric Key Cryptography: Same secret key used for encryption and decryption. Fast,) Tj
T*
(high throughput \(AES-256, ChaCha20\). Key exchange problem is major challenge.) Tj
T*
(- Asymmetric Key \(Public Key\) Cryptography: Mathematically linked key pair. Public key) Tj
T*
(encrypts data; matching Private key decrypts data \(RSA, Elliptic Curve Cryptography -) Tj
T*
(ECC\). Used for key exchange, digital signatures, SSL/TLS certificates.) Tj
T*
(- Cryptographic Hash Functions: One-way deterministic mathematical functions producing) Tj
T*
(fixed-size digest \(e.g., SHA-256\). Pre-image resistant, second pre-image resistant,) Tj
T*
(collision resistant.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 6.4 Common Cyber Threats, Attack Vectors and Defenses) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Malware Taxonomies: Viruses \(attach to host files\), Worms \(self-replicating across) Tj
T*
(networks without host intervention\), Trojan Horses \(disguised malicious utilities\),) Tj
T*
ET

