([PROBLEM 1: Containerization vs Virtual Machines Performance Benchmark]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Statement: An engineering team compares deploying 100 microservice instances) Tj
T*
(using Virtual Machines \(VMs running on a Type-1 hypervisor\) versus Docker containers on) Tj
T*
(Linux. Quantitatively compare: \(1\) Storage footprint, \(2\) Memory overhead, and \(3\)) Tj
T*
(Startup latency.) Tj
T*
(Architectural Performance Matrix:) Tj
T*
(* 1. Storage Footprint Comparison:) Tj
T*
(- VM Approach: Each VM requires a complete Guest OS image \(Ubuntu ~2.5 GB minimum\) plus) Tj
T*
(application \(~50 MB\). Total storage = 100 x 2.55 GB = 255 GB.) Tj
T*
(- Container Approach: Base Alpine/Debian image shared via union filesystem; application) Tj
T*
(image layer = ~60 MB. Total storage = ~2.5 GB base + \(100 x 60 MB delta\) = ~8.5 GB \(a) Tj
T*
(30x storage reduction!\).) Tj
T*
(* 2. RAM Memory Consumption:) Tj
T*
(- VM: Each Guest OS kernel and system daemons require minimum 512 MB reserved RAM before) Tj
T*
(application starts. Total idle RAM = 100 x 512 MB = 51.2 GB.) Tj
T*
(- Container: Shares single host kernel. Containers consume zero idle kernel overhead;) Tj
T*
(only active application memory \(~25 MB each\). Total RAM = 100 x 25 MB = 2.5 GB \(a 20x) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
